/* ============================================================================
   abundant-numbers-below.epher -- how many numbers beat their proper sum

   A number is abundant when its proper divisor sum exceeds it, that is
   sigma(n) > 2n (the Greek classification; Hardy and Wright, ch. 16,
   section 16.8). The script counts the abundant numbers from 2 up to
   1000 with a packed while loop (guide 1.30) around the classroom pair
   divisor sum, since epher has no divisor-sum builtin. The count up to
   and including 1000 is 246; strictly below 1000 there are 245, because
   1000 itself is abundant (its proper divisors add to 1340).

   Source: Hardy and Wright, "An Introduction to the Theory of Numbers",
   ch. 16, section 16.8. Results are exact; the recursion depth limits
   the def to n of a few thousand, plenty for the bound 1000.

   Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/abundant-numbers-below.epher"
   In the REPL:             load epher scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/abundant-numbers-below.epher
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   There is no knob: the bound 1000 is the point of the script.
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the proper-divisor-sum def ------------------------------------------------------
// pairdiv(n, i) adds each divisor of n with its partner n / i (a square
// adds its root once), so pairdiv(n, 1) is sigma(n); ps subtracts n.
def pairdiv(n, i) = if i * i > n then 0 else if mod(n, i) == 0 then (if i * i == n then i + pairdiv(n, i + 1) else i + n / i + pairdiv(n, i + 1)) else pairdiv(n, i + 1)
def sigma(n) = pairdiv(n, 1)
def ps(n) = sigma(n) - n

// ---- named checks ---------------------------------------------------------------------
print("proper divisor sum of 12:", ps(12))          // 16: the first abundant
print("12 is abundant:", ps(12) > 12)
print("proper divisor sum of 1000:", ps(1000))      // 1340: 1000 is abundant
print("8 is abundant:", ps(8) > 8)                  // false: 8 is deficient

// ---- the count --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {candidate, count}; every n with proper sum above n scores.
v = {2, 0}
while v[1] <= 1000 do v = {v[1] + 1, v[2] + (if ps(v[1]) > v[1] then 1 else 0)}
print("abundant numbers up to 1000:", v[2])         // 246, counting 1000
print("strictly below 1000:", v[2] - 1)             // 245: 1000 is one of them
/* ---- expected output ----
= proper divisor sum of 12: 16
= 12 is abundant: true
= proper divisor sum of 1000: 1340
= 8 is abundant: false
= {2, 0}
= abundant numbers up to 1000: 246
= strictly below 1000: 245
*/
